      "role": "user",
      "content": "Exactly. So if the utilitarian calculus clearly favors security as the foundation of a functional society, then isn't it intellectually dishonest to treat the \"privacy\" argument as being on equal footing? \n\nThe privacy argument usually relies on abstract notions of individual autonomy that break down the moment real lives are at stake. If the social contract is a trade-off where we give up certain liberties for protection, then increasing surveillance in a high-threat digital age isn't just a \"choice\"—it's the only rational way to maintain the contract itself.\n\nCan we really call it a \"debate\" anymore, or is it just a distinction between being realistic about modern threats and being idealistic to a fault?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
